        {
          "beat_type": "establishing",
          "description": "The gym shelter is introduced as an organized but improvised refuge — cots, handwritten signs, a folding table — grounding the crisis in community-scale human need.",
          "approximate_location": "opening",
          "confidence": 0.93
        },
        {
          "beat_type": "character introduction",
          "description": "Maya arrives in a soaked marching-band jacket with a flyer for her missing dog Comet, introducing a new character whose request is small and personal against the backdrop of large-scale emergency logistics.",
          "approximate_location": "early-middle",
          "confidence": 0.95
        },
        {
          "beat_type": "tonal contradiction / humanizing beat",
          "description": "June's immediate agreement to broadcast a missing dog alongside insulin drops and road closures — and Kell's mock-grumbling about interrupting the weather report — pairs the gravity of disaster relief with the mundane warmth of a lost pet, deliberately equalizing the personal and the critical.",
          "approximate_location": "middle",
          "confidence": 0.91
        },
        {
          "beat_type": "expository update",
          "description": "Noah's radio report about the east road and bridge crew provides a logistical update that June records, maintaining the operational thread of the story.",
          "approximate_location": "late-middle",
          "confidence": 0.9
        },
        {
          "beat_type": "closing character moment",
          "description": "Maya spells out Comet's name and defines it ('Like the fast kind of star'), ending the scene on a small, specific human detail that anchors the emotional stakes of the larger crisis.",
